<worksheet><title>Methods of Enumeration (1.2)</title>
 <page>
    <p>
      <alert>Multiplication Principle:</alert> Suppose 2 experiments are
      performed, <m>E_{1}</m> and <m>E_{2}</m>. If <m>E_{1}</m> has <m>n_{1}</m>
      possible outcomes and <m>E_{2}</m> has <m>n_{2}</m> possible outcomes,
      then the composite experiment of performing <m>E_{1}</m> followed by
      <m>E_{2}</m> has <fillin characters="12"/> possible outcomes.
    </p>
<exercise>
<introduction>
    <p>
      <em>Example</em> Let <m>E_{1}</m> be the experiment of selecting at random
      a pair of socks out of your drawer containing yellow(y), pink(p) and
      white(w) socks. Let <m>E_{2}</m> be the experiment of selecting either
      black(bl) or grey(gr) shoes. Then the sample space for the experiment of
      doing <m>E_{1}</m> followed by <m>E_{2}</m> is all (sock,shoe) possible
      combinations. 
    </p>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
     <p>
        List all outcomes in the sample space:
     </p>
</statement>
</task>
<task workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
           Alternatively we could model it with a tree diagram:  
        </p>
    </statement>
</task>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="0.5in">
<statement>
    <p>
      <alert>General Multiplication rule:</alert> If experiments
      <m>E_{1}, E_{2}, E_{3}, ..., E_{k}</m> has <m>n_{1}, n_{2}, ..., n_{k}</m>
      outcomes respectively after previous experiments have been performed, then
      the composite experiment of performing <m>E_{1}</m> followed by
      <m>E_{2}</m>, ... <m>E_{k}</m> has the following number of possible
      outcomes:
    </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
     <em>Examples:</em> 
    </p>
  </introduction>
 <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
            How many different <m>7-</m> digit phone numbers are possible if the
            first number cannot be a <m>0</m> or <m>1</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        How many different license plates of the form LETTER LETTER LETTER
            DIGIT DIGIT DIGIT are there?
      </p>
    </statement>
    </task>
   
     <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
          <p>
           I have <m>4</m> different pictures to hang on the wall. In how many
            different ways can I arrange them in a row?
          </p>
          </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
            In a class of <m>30</m> students, how many ways are there to elect a
            president, vice president, and treasurer? (Assume no student can hold more than one office.)
          </p>
        </statement>
    </task> 
  </exercise>
 
    <p>
      More generally suppose we want to arrange <m>n</m> different things in a
      row. How many ways?
    </p>

     <md>
    \begin{array}{ccccc}\underline{\hspace{.75in}} &amp; \underline{\hspace{.75in}} &amp; \underline{\hspace{.75in}} &amp; \cdots &amp; \underline{\hspace{.75in}} \\ 1^{\text{st}} &amp; 2^{\text{nd}} &amp; 3^{\text{rd}} &amp; \cdots &amp; n^{\text{th}}\\\end{array}
  </md>

<definition xml:id="def-permutation">
<title>Permutation (B:1.2.1)</title>
<statement>
    <p>
      A <alert> permutation </alert>  is an arrangement of objects in a
      row where order matters.
    </p>
</statement>
</definition>
</page>
<page>
<exercise workspace=".5in">
  <statement>
    <p> The total number of permutations on <m>n</m> different objects is:</p>
      </statement>
</exercise>
 <exercise workspace="1in">
  <statement>
    <p>
       If we had <m>n</m> different things and wanted to arrange just <m>r</m> of them in a
      row, we'd have this many ways:
       </p>
  <md>
    \begin{array}{ccccc}\underline{\hspace{1in}} &amp; \underline{\hspace{1in}} &amp; \underline{\hspace{1in}} &amp; \cdots &amp; \underline{\hspace{1in}} \\ 1^{\text{st}} &amp; 2^{\text{nd}} &amp; 3^{\text{rd}} &amp; \cdots &amp; r^{\text{th}}\\\end{array}
  </md>
    
    <p>We denote the number of such arrangements by</p>
  </statement>
 </exercise>
   
<definition xml:id="perm-on-n-objects">
<title>(B:1.2-2)</title>


<statement>
    <p>
      Each of the <m>_{n}P_{r}</m> arrangements is
      called a <em>permutation on <m>n</m> objects taken <m>r</m> at a time.</em>
    </p>
</statement>
</definition>

      <exercise workspace=".5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            There are <m>10</m> people in a race. How many finishing
      orders are there? <!--10!\\-->
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
 <exercise workspace=".5in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      There are <m>10</m> people in a race. How many ways to
      award first, second, and third place?
    </p>
  </statement>
 </exercise>

<definition xml:id ="ordered-sample">
<title>(B:1.2-3)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
       If <m>r</m> objects are selected from a set of
      <m>n</m> objects, and if the order of the selection is noted, then the
      selected set of <m>r</m> objects is called
    </p>
     <p>
      <fillin characters="100"/> <!--an {\bf ordered sample of size $r$}.\\-->
    </p>

    </statement>
  </definition>
   </page>
   <page>
<definition xml:id="sampling-with-without-replacement">
<title>(B:1.2-4/5)</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
      <alert>Sampling with replacement</alert>
      occurs when an object is selected and then replaced before the next object
      is selected. <alert>Sampling without replacement</alert> occurs when an
      object is not replaced after it has been selected.
    </p>
</statement>
</definition>
    <p>
      <em>Examples:</em>
    </p>
<exercise workspace=".5in">
<statement>
    <p>
      A state lottery selects <m>3</m> balls numbered <m>0-9</m>
      one at a time. After each ball is selected, it is put back. How many
      possible outcomes are there? (Assume order matters)
    </p>
</statement>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace=".5in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose in the above lottery the balls are not put back
      after being selected. How many possible outcomes are there? (Assume order
      matters)
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

    <p>
      We note that given <m>n</m> different objects, the number of
      <em>ordered</em> samples of size <m>r</m> is:
    </p>

    <p>
      with replacement: <fillin characters="10"/> <!--$n^r$ (multiplication principle)\\-->
    </p>

    <p>
      without replacement: <fillin characters="10"/>
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>Example - Unordered sets</em>
    </p>
