<definition xml:id="unordered-subset">
<title>(B:1.2-6)</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
       Each of the <m>_{n}C_{r}</m> unordered subsets
      is called a
    </p>

    <p>
      <fillin characters="100"/>
    </p>
</statement>
</definition>
    <p>
      <em>Examples:</em>
    </p>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A committee of <m>3</m> is to be chosen from a group of <m>5</m>
            people - how many ways?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
     
     <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
           How many different ways are there to deal a <m>5-</m> card hand from
            a standard <m>52</m> card deck?
        </p>
      </statement>
     </exercise>
</page>
  
<page>
    <p>
      <alert>Back to probability.</alert> Let <m>N(A)</m> be the number of
      outcomes in event <m>A</m>. If all outcomes in a sample space are equally
      likely, then the probability of an event <m>A</m> is
      <m>\displaystyle{P(A)=\frac{N(A)}{N(S)}}</m>. So knowing how to count
      helps us compute probabilities.
    </p>
<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement> 
          <p>
            A committee of <m>3</m> is to be chosen from
            <m>\{Alice (A), Bob (B), Chuck (C), David (D), Edgar (E) \}</m>.
            What is the probability that <m>AB</m> will be chosen?
          </p>
        </statement>
        </exercise>
<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
          <p>
            What is the probability that a random <m>5</m>-card hand (from a
            standard <m>52-</m> card deck) consists of all diamonds?
          </p>
        </statement>
</exercise>
<p><alert>Notation</alert>
    </p>
   <ul>
    <li>
      <p> The number of permutations on <m>n</m> distinct objects taken <m>r</m> at a time is denoted by <m>_{n}P_{r}</m> or <m>P(n,r)</m> and is given by
      <m>_{n}P_{r} = \frac{n!}{(n-r)!}</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p> The number of combinations (unordered sets) of <m>n</m> distinct objects taken <m>r</m> at a time is denoted by <m>_{n}C_{r}</m>, <m>C(n,r)</m>, or <m>\binom{n}{r}</m> (read "n choose r") and is given by
      <m>_{n}C_{r} = \frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}</m>.
      </p>
    </li>
   </ul>
  </page>
  
</worksheet>